use console.println
use clock.now

/// What `String.contains` costs, at nine combinations of receiver length,
/// needle length and match position — and what the same nine cost written in
/// Cove.
///
/// `String.contains` **was** `Intrinsic::StringContains` when this package was
/// written, and these rows are two migrations of history. It was the operation
/// whose measurement would justify a bounded run-search primitive, and
/// [issue #442](https://github.com/myuon/cove/issues/442) is the reason the
/// package exists: the intrinsic's arm called `operand::text` **twice**, and
/// that helper allocated a `Vec`, filled it one byte at a time, and validated
/// the result as UTF-8 — per operand, per call. So the intrinsic arm of every
/// intrinsic-against-Cove ratio carried a term that scaled with the *operands*
/// rather than with the work, and a primitive justified against it would have
/// been justified against an artefact.
///
/// [PR #446](https://github.com/myuon/cove/pull/446) removed the copy, which
/// moved the row from 246.4 ns to 99.9 ns and flipped the sign of the native
/// comparison; and
/// [ADR 0065](../../docs/adr/0065-a-run-search-is-the-one-loop-that-stays-below.md)
/// then took the operation out of `Intrinsic` altogether. `String.contains` is
/// `std.string.contains` now — a comparison against -1 over one
/// `Inst::RunFind`, an IR instruction the VM dispatches directly and both code
/// generators lower to a runtime helper. So the first column measures the
/// instruction where it measured a mediated intrinsic call, which is exactly
/// what ADR 0065's third adoption gate asks for: the dispatch cost of the new
/// instruction against the 46–49 ns an `Inst::IntrinsicCall` costs to be one.
///
/// # Nine rows, three axes
///
/// `benches/endswith` and `benches/startswith` need four rows because their
/// loops have two axes — receiver length and needle length — and answer on
/// the lengths alone when the needle is the longer. A search has a third:
/// *where* the match is, which decides how many start positions the scan
/// tries before it stops. So:
///
/// - **empty** is the empty needle, which every implementation answers `true`
///   to without reading a byte — the cheapest answer `contains` has;
/// - **needleLong** is the refusal on length, 16 bytes against 50, and no
///   byte of either read;
/// - **start**, **middle** and **end** are the same six-byte needle in the
///   same eighty-eight-byte receiver at offsets 0, 50 and 82, so the only
///   thing that differs between them is how far the scan walked;
/// - **noMatch** is the same receiver and a six-byte needle that is not in
///   it: eighty-three start positions tried and rejected, the worst case;
/// - **shortHit** and **shortMiss** are the first-position hit and the whole
///   scan over the sixteen-byte receiver, which is what says how much of a
///   row is the per-call constant;
/// - **longNeedle** is fifty bytes matched at position 0, where the
///   per-compared-byte slope shows up against that constant.
///
/// # Three arms over the same inputs in the same process
///
/// Each row is run three ways:
///
/// - **runFind** — `text.contains(needle)`, which is `std.string.contains`:
///   one `core.stringFind`, which is one `Inst::RunFind`, and a comparison of
///   its answer against -1. It was an `Inst::IntrinsicCall` into
///   `str::contains` over two freshly materialised `String`s when this file
///   was written, and the column's name is what changed underneath it;
/// - **scalar** — [`containsScalar`], which is `benches/stringlib`'s body
///   verbatim: a scan over `codePointAtByte`, comparing one Unicode scalar at
///   a time. It is the Cove `contains` this repository already has, and it is
///   the one ADR 0064's 4.02x figure is about;
/// - **byte** — [`containsByte`], the same scan over `byteAt`, which is what
///   `std.string.startsWith` and `std.string.endsWith` were migrated to, and
///   what `contains` would have been migrated to had a bounded comparison and
///   a whole-haystack search been the same shape. It is here because `byteAt` lowers to `Inst::RunLoad` — an IR
///   instruction, not a call — where `codePointAtByte` is a Cove function
///   assembling an `Option`, so the two Cove arms are a factor apart and only
///   one of them is what a `contains` migration would look like. A byte-wise
///   scan cannot find a match that is not there: UTF-8 is self-synchronising,
///   so a valid needle's first byte never equals a continuation byte.
///
/// Running all three over the same strings in one process is the point. A
/// ratio taken across two programs is a ratio plus a code layout, and ADR
/// 0063 measured that layout at more than the difference being looked for.
///
/// # Two turn counts, and why
///
/// [`turns`] is 200,000, `benches/endswith`'s and `benches/startswith`'s. The
/// Cove arms run [`coveTurns`], a fortieth of that, and the rows are reported
/// as nanoseconds per call either way so they stay comparable.
///
/// The reason is `crates/cove-cli/tests/vm_coverage.rs`, which runs every
/// program in this repository on the tree-walking interpreter as well as on
/// the linear-memory backend. `endswith`'s most expensive row is fifty byte
/// comparisons a turn; **noMatch** on the scalar arm is eighty-three start
/// positions, each decoding a scalar out of each string, which is more than
/// an order of magnitude more work per turn than anything those two packages
/// do. That is measured rather than assumed: at 20,000 Cove turns this
/// program took 78s on `--backend ast`, of which 68s was the scalar arm,
/// against 13.8s for `benches/endswith` and 93.3s for `benches/keyed` — the
/// most expensive program in the repository. A fortieth puts it at about 20s,
/// between `benches/seqsearch` and `benches/endswith`, and the rows it costs
/// resolution on are the two cheapest, where a microsecond of clock
/// quantisation is still under a nanosecond per call.
///
/// # The loops are in callees, and they are padded
///
/// The outermost frame is always encoded, so a loop written in `main` is a VM
/// measurement even under `--backend native`. Each row is therefore a call to
/// a counting callee whose body is the loop, and `main` does nothing in a turn
/// but read a clock either side of it.
///
/// That holds only while the callee is too big to be inlined into [`row`],
/// which the native tier refuses for the `now()` at its start.
/// `cove_ir::lower::inline`'s cold `LIMIT` is sixteen instructions and its
/// `HOT_LIMIT` is forty-eight, and [`countContains`] is a `while` around one
/// `RunFind` — about ten. `benches/endswith` records what happens then:
/// its "native" column was the encoded VM wearing the native tier's name, for
/// a whole baseline. So every counting callee here opens with [`ballast`], a
/// block the parameter's sign makes unreachable and nothing can fold away,
/// sized past `HOT_LIMIT` rather than past `LIMIT` so that the answer does not
/// depend on which kind of site called it. It costs nothing at run time and
/// it is checked rather than assumed: `--backend native --boundary` names the
/// compiled and the refused, and the only refusals are [`row`] and `main`,
/// each for the `now()` in it.
fn turns() -> Int {
  200000
}

/// A fortieth of [`turns`], for the two Cove arms. See the note above.
fn coveTurns() -> Int {
  5000
}

/// Sixteen bytes. `benches/startswith`'s short receiver.
fn shortText() -> String {
  "crates/main.cove"
}

/// Eighty-eight bytes. `benches/startswith`'s long receiver.
fn longText() -> String {
  "crates/cove-runtime/src/vm/intrinsics/text/deeply/nested/directory/tree/module/main.cove"
}

/// Six bytes, at offset 0 of both receivers.
fn atStart() -> String {
  "crates"
}

/// Six bytes, at offset 50 of [`longText`].
fn atMiddle() -> String {
  "nested"
}

/// Six bytes, at offset 82 of [`longText`] — its last six.
fn atEnd() -> String {
  "n.cove"
}

/// Six bytes, in neither receiver.
fn absent() -> String {
  "zzzzzz"
}

/// Fifty bytes, at offset 0 of [`longText`] and longer than [`shortText`].
fn longNeedle() -> String {
  "crates/cove-runtime/src/vm/intrinsics/text/deeply/"
}

fn empty() -> String {
  ""
}

/// The number of UTF-8 bytes the scalar `codePoint` occupies.
///
/// A `String` is valid UTF-8 in its shortest form, so the width is a function
/// of the value. `benches/stringlib`'s, verbatim.
fn width(codePoint: Int) -> Int {
  if codePoint < 128 {
    1
  } else if codePoint < 2048 {
    2
  } else if codePoint < 65536 {
    3
  } else {
    4
  }
}

/// Whether `needle`'s scalars run from byte `start` of `text`.
///
/// `benches/stringlib`'s, verbatim, including its reason for going scalar by
/// scalar: `codePointAtByte` answers `None` inside a character, so two
/// different characters' continuation bytes compare equal to one another and
/// a byte-wise loop over *this* primitive would find matches that are not
/// there.
fn runsFrom(text: String, needle: String, start: Int, needleEnd: Int) -> Bool {
  var t = start
  var n = 0
  while n < needleEnd {
    match needle.codePointAtByte(n) {
      Some(want) => {
        match text.codePointAtByte(t) {
          Some(got) => {
            if got != want {
              return false
            }
            t += width(got)
            n += width(want)
          }
          None => {
            return false
          }
        }
      }
      None => {
        return false
      }
    }
  }
  true
}

/// `String.contains`, scanning scalars. `benches/stringlib`'s, verbatim.
fn containsScalar(text: String, needle: String) -> Bool {
  let needleEnd = needle.byteLength()
  if needleEnd == 0 {
    return true
  }
  let end = text.byteLength()
  var at = 0
  while at + needleEnd <= end {
    if runsFrom(text, needle, at, needleEnd) {
      return true
    }
    match text.codePointAtByte(at) {
      Some(codePoint) => {
        at += width(codePoint)
      }
      None => {
        at += 1
      }
    }
  }
  false
}

/// `String.contains`, scanning bytes — `std.string.startsWith`'s loop at every
/// start position.
///
/// Not `benches/stringlib`'s; this arm is the one a Phase 1 migration would
/// actually be written as, because `byteAt` lowers to `Inst::RunLoad` and
/// `codePointAtByte` lowers to a Cove call that builds an `Option`. It is
/// correct for the same reason the two migrated predicates are: UTF-8 is
/// self-synchronising, so a valid needle's bytes cannot match starting inside
/// a character.
fn containsByte(text: String, needle: String) -> Bool {
  let needleEnd = needle.byteLength()
  if needleEnd == 0 {
    return true
  }
  let end = text.byteLength()
  var at = 0
  while at + needleEnd <= end {
    var n = 0
    var same = true
    while n < needleEnd {
      if text.byteAt(at + n) != needle.byteAt(n) {
        same = false
        n = needleEnd
      } else {
        n += 1
      }
    }
    if same {
      return true
    }
    at += 1
  }
  false
}

/// Instructions a counting callee carries so that it is not expanded into
/// [`row`], which the native tier refuses.
///
/// `rounds` is never negative at any call site, so none of this runs; nothing
/// in the lowering can prove that, so none of it is folded away either. See
/// the module note — the size is past `HOT_LIMIT`, not merely past `LIMIT`.
fn ballast(rounds: Int) -> Int {
  var dead = 0
  if rounds < 0 {
    dead = dead + 1
    dead = dead + 2
    dead = dead + 3
    dead = dead + 4
    dead = dead + 5
    dead = dead + 6
    dead = dead + 7
    dead = dead + 8
    dead = dead + 9
    dead = dead + 10
    dead = dead + 11
    dead = dead + 12
    dead = dead + 13
    dead = dead + 14
    dead = dead + 15
    dead = dead + 16
    dead = dead + 17
    dead = dead + 18
    dead = dead + 19
    dead = dead + 20
    dead = dead + 21
    dead = dead + 22
    dead = dead + 23
    dead = dead + 24
  }
  dead
}

/// `rounds` calls of `String.contains` and nothing else the loop can be about.
///
/// One `Inst::RunFind` a turn since ADR 0065, where it was one
/// `Inst::IntrinsicCall` a turn before it — the same call site, the same
/// strings, and one dispatch either way.
fn countContains(text: String, needle: String, rounds: Int) -> Int {
  var found = ballast(rounds)
  var i = 0
  if rounds < 0 {
    found = found + 1
    found = found + 2
    found = found + 3
    found = found + 4
    found = found + 5
    found = found + 6
    found = found + 7
    found = found + 8
    found = found + 9
    found = found + 10
    found = found + 11
    found = found + 12
    found = found + 13
    found = found + 14
    found = found + 15
    found = found + 16
    found = found + 17
    found = found + 18
    found = found + 19
    found = found + 20
    found = found + 21
    found = found + 22
    found = found + 23
    found = found + 24
  }
  while i < rounds {
    if text.contains(needle) {
      found = found + 1
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  found
}

/// `rounds` calls of [`containsScalar`].
fn countScalar(text: String, needle: String, rounds: Int) -> Int {
  var found = ballast(rounds)
  var i = 0
  if rounds < 0 {
    found = found + 1
    found = found + 2
    found = found + 3
    found = found + 4
    found = found + 5
    found = found + 6
    found = found + 7
    found = found + 8
    found = found + 9
    found = found + 10
    found = found + 11
    found = found + 12
    found = found + 13
    found = found + 14
    found = found + 15
    found = found + 16
    found = found + 17
    found = found + 18
    found = found + 19
    found = found + 20
    found = found + 21
    found = found + 22
    found = found + 23
    found = found + 24
  }
  while i < rounds {
    if containsScalar(text, needle) {
      found = found + 1
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  found
}

/// `rounds` calls of [`containsByte`].
fn countByte(text: String, needle: String, rounds: Int) -> Int {
  var found = ballast(rounds)
  var i = 0
  if rounds < 0 {
    found = found + 1
    found = found + 2
    found = found + 3
    found = found + 4
    found = found + 5
    found = found + 6
    found = found + 7
    found = found + 8
    found = found + 9
    found = found + 10
    found = found + 11
    found = found + 12
    found = found + 13
    found = found + 14
    found = found + 15
    found = found + 16
    found = found + 17
    found = found + 18
    found = found + 19
    found = found + 20
    found = found + 21
    found = found + 22
    found = found + 23
    found = found + 24
  }
  while i < rounds {
    if containsByte(text, needle) {
      found = found + 1
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  found
}

/// The counting callees' loop with the call taken out, and its branch taken
/// every turn.
///
/// Six of the nine rows answer `true`; the three that answer `false` do one
/// addition a turn less than this does, and are reported cheap by exactly
/// that much. On the native tier a row can therefore read *negative*, which
/// is not a broken clock but "too cheap to separate from the loop".
fn countBare(rounds: Int) -> Int {
  var found = ballast(rounds)
  var i = 0
  if rounds < 0 {
    found = found + 1
    found = found + 2
    found = found + 3
    found = found + 4
    found = found + 5
    found = found + 6
    found = found + 7
    found = found + 8
    found = found + 9
    found = found + 10
    found = found + 11
    found = found + 12
    found = found + 13
    found = found + 14
    found = found + 15
    found = found + 16
    found = found + 17
    found = found + 18
    found = found + 19
    found = found + 20
    found = found + 21
    found = found + 22
    found = found + 23
    found = found + 24
  }
  while i < rounds {
    if i < rounds {
      found = found + 1
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  found
}

/// How long `rounds` turns took, in microseconds, and how many answered
/// `true`.
struct Row {
  micros: Int
  found: Int
}

fn runFindRow(text: String, needle: String, rounds: Int) -> Row {
  let from = now()
  let found = countContains(text, needle, rounds)
  Row(micros: (now() - from).micros(), found: found)
}

fn scalarRow(text: String, needle: String, rounds: Int) -> Row {
  let from = now()
  let found = countScalar(text, needle, rounds)
  Row(micros: (now() - from).micros(), found: found)
}

fn byteRow(text: String, needle: String, rounds: Int) -> Row {
  let from = now()
  let found = countByte(text, needle, rounds)
  Row(micros: (now() - from).micros(), found: found)
}

/// What one call cost, in nanoseconds, over `rounds` turns of which `bare`
/// microseconds were the loop itself.
fn perCall(micros: Int, bare: Int, rounds: Int) -> Int {
  (micros - bare) * 1000 / rounds
}

export fn main() -> Result<Unit, Error> {
  let rounds = turns()
  let coveRounds = coveTurns()

  let bareFrom = now()
  let bare = countBare(rounds)
  let bareMicros = (now() - bareFrom).micros()

  let coveBareFrom = now()
  let coveBare = countBare(coveRounds)
  let coveBareMicros = (now() - coveBareFrom).micros()

  let iEmpty = runFindRow(shortText(), empty(), rounds)
  let iNeedleLong = runFindRow(shortText(), longNeedle(), rounds)
  let iStart = runFindRow(longText(), atStart(), rounds)
  let iMiddle = runFindRow(longText(), atMiddle(), rounds)
  let iEnd = runFindRow(longText(), atEnd(), rounds)
  let iNoMatch = runFindRow(longText(), absent(), rounds)
  let iShortHit = runFindRow(shortText(), atStart(), rounds)
  let iShortMiss = runFindRow(shortText(), absent(), rounds)
  let iLongNeedle = runFindRow(longText(), longNeedle(), rounds)

  let sEmpty = scalarRow(shortText(), empty(), coveRounds)
  let sNeedleLong = scalarRow(shortText(), longNeedle(), coveRounds)
  let sStart = scalarRow(longText(), atStart(), coveRounds)
  let sMiddle = scalarRow(longText(), atMiddle(), coveRounds)
  let sEnd = scalarRow(longText(), atEnd(), coveRounds)
  let sNoMatch = scalarRow(longText(), absent(), coveRounds)
  let sShortHit = scalarRow(shortText(), atStart(), coveRounds)
  let sShortMiss = scalarRow(shortText(), absent(), coveRounds)
  let sLongNeedle = scalarRow(longText(), longNeedle(), coveRounds)

  let bEmpty = byteRow(shortText(), empty(), coveRounds)
  let bNeedleLong = byteRow(shortText(), longNeedle(), coveRounds)
  let bStart = byteRow(longText(), atStart(), coveRounds)
  let bMiddle = byteRow(longText(), atMiddle(), coveRounds)
  let bEnd = byteRow(longText(), atEnd(), coveRounds)
  let bNoMatch = byteRow(longText(), absent(), coveRounds)
  let bShortHit = byteRow(shortText(), atStart(), coveRounds)
  let bShortMiss = byteRow(shortText(), absent(), coveRounds)
  let bLongNeedle = byteRow(longText(), longNeedle(), coveRounds)

  // What each row answered, checked before any of it is read as a timing: a
  // row that stopped matching would still report a number, and the number
  // would be of something else. The three arms are checked against each
  // other's counts as well as against the expected one, which is what makes
  // this a differential rather than three separate assertions.
  assertEqual(bare, rounds)?
  assertEqual(coveBare, coveRounds)?

  assertEqual(iEmpty.found, rounds)?
  assertEqual(iNeedleLong.found, 0)?
  assertEqual(iStart.found, rounds)?
  assertEqual(iMiddle.found, rounds)?
  assertEqual(iEnd.found, rounds)?
  assertEqual(iNoMatch.found, 0)?
  assertEqual(iShortHit.found, rounds)?
  assertEqual(iShortMiss.found, 0)?
  assertEqual(iLongNeedle.found, rounds)?

  assertEqual(sEmpty.found, coveRounds)?
  assertEqual(sNeedleLong.found, 0)?
  assertEqual(sStart.found, coveRounds)?
  assertEqual(sMiddle.found, coveRounds)?
  assertEqual(sEnd.found, coveRounds)?
  assertEqual(sNoMatch.found, 0)?
  assertEqual(sShortHit.found, coveRounds)?
  assertEqual(sShortMiss.found, 0)?
  assertEqual(sLongNeedle.found, coveRounds)?

  assertEqual(bEmpty.found, coveRounds)?
  assertEqual(bNeedleLong.found, 0)?
  assertEqual(bStart.found, coveRounds)?
  assertEqual(bMiddle.found, coveRounds)?
  assertEqual(bEnd.found, coveRounds)?
  assertEqual(bNoMatch.found, 0)?
  assertEqual(bShortHit.found, coveRounds)?
  assertEqual(bShortMiss.found, 0)?
  assertEqual(bLongNeedle.found, coveRounds)?

  println(
    "bare loop                          {bareMicros * 1000 / rounds} ns/turn",
  )?
  println("bare loop (cove counts)            {coveBareMicros * 1000 / coveRounds} ns/turn")?
  println("")?
  println("row                 recv needle at    runFind     scalar       byte")?
  println("empty                 16      0  -  {perCall(iEmpty.micros, bareMicros, rounds)} {perCall(sEmpty.micros, coveBareMicros, coveRounds)} {perCall(bEmpty.micros, coveBareMicros, coveRounds)}")?
  println("needleLong            16     50  -  {perCall(iNeedleLong.micros, bareMicros, rounds)} {perCall(sNeedleLong.micros, coveBareMicros, coveRounds)} {perCall(bNeedleLong.micros, coveBareMicros, coveRounds)}")?
  println("start                 88      6  0  {perCall(iStart.micros, bareMicros, rounds)} {perCall(sStart.micros, coveBareMicros, coveRounds)} {perCall(bStart.micros, coveBareMicros, coveRounds)}")?
  println("middle                88      6 50  {perCall(iMiddle.micros, bareMicros, rounds)} {perCall(sMiddle.micros, coveBareMicros, coveRounds)} {perCall(bMiddle.micros, coveBareMicros, coveRounds)}")?
  println("end                   88      6 82  {perCall(iEnd.micros, bareMicros, rounds)} {perCall(sEnd.micros, coveBareMicros, coveRounds)} {perCall(bEnd.micros, coveBareMicros, coveRounds)}")?
  println("noMatch               88      6  -  {perCall(iNoMatch.micros, bareMicros, rounds)} {perCall(sNoMatch.micros, coveBareMicros, coveRounds)} {perCall(bNoMatch.micros, coveBareMicros, coveRounds)}")?
  println("shortHit              16      6  0  {perCall(iShortHit.micros, bareMicros, rounds)} {perCall(sShortHit.micros, coveBareMicros, coveRounds)} {perCall(bShortHit.micros, coveBareMicros, coveRounds)}")?
  println("shortMiss             16      6  -  {perCall(iShortMiss.micros, bareMicros, rounds)} {perCall(sShortMiss.micros, coveBareMicros, coveRounds)} {perCall(bShortMiss.micros, coveBareMicros, coveRounds)}")?
  println("longNeedle            88     50  0  {perCall(iLongNeedle.micros, bareMicros, rounds)} {perCall(sLongNeedle.micros, coveBareMicros, coveRounds)} {perCall(bLongNeedle.micros, coveBareMicros, coveRounds)}")?
  Ok(())
}
